  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 241 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nSaturday through Thursday:\n\nExpect breezy southerly winds on Saturday with gusts of 25 to 30\nmph in the afternoon. The wind direction has trended a little \nmore south than southwest, which means that areas right along \nthe lakeshore should remain cooler due to a slight onshore \ncomponent (lake- induced). Very warm temps are expected due to \npersistent and dry southwest flow originating from the western \nstates. This is due to a shift from ridging over the Pacific \nNorthwest to a broad trough and a resultant surface low over \nthe Northern Plains, and high pressure over the northeastern \nStates. With 925mb temps of 20 to 21C over southern WI and \nefficient mixing of a very dry air mass right up to that level \n(and likely a weak inversion above that), we can expect the high\ntemps to be right around 80 (up to 82). The record high for \nthat day is 84. \n\nWith the warm temps and dewpoints in the lower 50s (with\npotential for lower due to mixing), the min RH values will be in\nthe mid 40 percent range. While this is not critical and we are\nstill plenty green, fire weather is not a major concern, but\nalso not something to be ignored. \n\nHurricane Isaias over the Gulf of America will track northward\nand then just south of the Ohio River on Sunday (slightly\nfarther south than previous model runs). Southeast WI will \nlikely see cloud cover from this system (but no rain), which \nwill keep temps in the lower- mid 70s near Lake Michigan instead\nof upper 70s).\n\nA northern stream mid level trough is expected to slide across\nUS/Canadian border and Lake Superior Monday or Tuesday (could be\na series of waves) and this will push a cold front down through\ncentral and eventually southern WI. Showers and perhaps a few\nthunderstorms will likely accompany this front, but the timing\nand rainfall amounts are still very uncertain. \n\nCooler, more seasonable temperatures will return around the\nmiddle of next week as high pressure moves over the Upper Great\nLakes.The next low approaching from the Northern Plains will\nbring our next chance for rain to southern WI on a warm frontal\nboundary Wednesday afternoon and night into Thursday. \n\nCronce",
